        {
          "title": "Regression Mental Model",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Linear regression",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Predicts a ==number==: ŷ = w·x + b",
                "A **weighted sum** of features plus a ==bias==",
                "Learning = choose w and b that ==minimize a loss== over the training data",
                "Residual = y − ŷ; the fit line has the smallest ==Σ residual²=="
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Logistic regression",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "z = w·x + b",
                    "sub": "linear score",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "p = σ(z)",
                    "sub": "probability",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "p ≥ 0.5 ?",
                    "sub": "threshold",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Class",
                    "sub": "0 or 1",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Linear",
                "Logistic"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Predicts",
                  "a number",
                  "a class probability"
                ],
                [
                  "Formula",
                  "ŷ = w·x + b",
                  "p = σ(w·x + b)"
                ],
                [
                  "Last step",
                  "none",
                  "threshold (default 0.5)"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Both are linear models",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Boundary is where p = 0.5, i.e. ==w·x + b = 0==",
                "A straight **line** in 2D, a ==hyperplane== in higher dimensions",
                "Class flips as w·x + b changes sign"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "The sigmoid bends the **probability**, not the boundary. Logistic regression still separates classes with a ==flat line or plane==."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Least Squares and Gradient Descent",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Least Squares and Gradient Descent",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Least Squares and the Normal Equation",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Least squares minimizes ==Σ(y − ŷ)²==, the sum of squared residuals",
                "Closed form: {green:w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy}",
                "Cost about ==O(n·d²+d³)==: fine for **small d**",
                "{red:Fails} when XᵀX is singular (collinear features)"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Gradient Descent",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Predict ŷ",
                    "sub": "current w",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gradient",
                    "sub": "∂L/∂w",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Update",
                    "sub": "w ← w − α·∂L/∂w",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Loss falling?",
                    "sub": "repeat until it stops",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "α (learning rate)",
                "Effect"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Too large",
                  "{red:diverges}"
                ],
                [
                  "Too small",
                  "{blue:crawls}"
                ],
                [
                  "Just right + standardized features",
                  "{green:converges fast}"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Batch, SGD, Mini-batch",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Variant",
                "Rows per step"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Batch",
                  "all rows"
                ],
                [
                  "SGD",
                  "one row"
                ],
                [
                  "Mini-batch",
                  "a small chunk (==default in practice==)"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Always ==standardize features== before gradient descent. Use the normal equation only for small d."
            },
            {
              "type": "sidebox",
              "title": "Interview Point",
              "items": [
                "Why can the normal equation fail? XᵀX is singular with collinear features.",
                "Why standardize before gradient descent?",
                "What happens if α is too large or too small?",
                "Batch vs SGD vs mini-batch?"
              ],
              "ink": "orange"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sigmoid and Logistic Regression",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Sigmoid and Logistic Regression",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Sigmoid and logit",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^−z) squashes any real z into ==(0, 1)==",
                "σ(0) = 0.5",
                "Logit = ln(p / (1 − p)) = w·x + b: linear in ==log-odds=="
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Reading a weight",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "w = change in log-odds per one-unit increase in a feature",
                "e^w = ==odds ratio== for that one-unit increase"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "w",
                "e^w",
                "Effect"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "0",
                  "1",
                  "none"
                ],
                [
                  "0.69",
                  "2",
                  "odds double"
                ],
                [
                  "−0.69",
                  "0.5",
                  "odds halve"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Training and threshold",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Log loss = −(1/n)·Σ[y·ln p + (1−y)·ln(1−p)]",
                "Gradient w.r.t. w: ==Xᵀ(p − y)/n==, same form as linear regression"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Threshold",
                "Favors",
                "Cost"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Below 0.5",
                  "recall: positives caught",
                  "more false positives"
                ],
                [
                  "Above 0.5",
                  "precision: positives correct",
                  "more misses"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Separable data",
              "text": "Weights grow without bound unless ==regularized==."
            },
            {
              "type": "sidebox",
              "title": "Interview Point",
              "items": [
                "**What is e^w?** Odds ratio per unit.",
                "**Lower the threshold?** More recall, more false positives."
              ],
              "ink": "orange"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Loss Functions: MSE and Log Loss",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Loss Functions: MSE and Log Loss",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. MSE and Its Cousins",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==MSE== = (1/n)·Σ(y − ŷ)²",
                "**Convex** for linear regression",
                "Big errors cost {red:quadratically}; outliers dominate"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "MSE",
                "MAE",
                "Huber"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Penalty",
                  "squared",
                  "absolute",
                  "squared small, linear large"
                ],
                [
                  "Outliers",
                  "sensitive",
                  "robust",
                  "robust"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Log Loss",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Log loss== = binary cross-entropy: −(1/n)·Σ[y·ln p + (1−y)·ln(1−p)]",
                "y=1 keeps `ln p`; y=0 keeps `ln(1−p)`",
                "Confident and wrong costs a lot: p = 0.01, y = 1 → −ln 0.01 ≈ 4.6"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "from math import log",
                "def log_loss(ys, ps, e=1e-15):",
                "    ps = [min(max(p, e), 1 - e) for p in ps]",
                "    return -sum(y*log(p) + (1-y)*log(1-p) for y, p in zip(ys, ps))/len(ys)",
                "print(round(log_loss([1], [0.01]), 2))"
              ],
              "note": "clipping to [ε, 1−ε] avoids ln 0 = −∞"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "output",
              "lines": [
                "4.61"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "sidebox",
              "title": "Rules",
              "items": [
                "{red:Never} MSE for logistic: sigmoid makes it non-convex, weak gradients",
                "Log loss is convex",
                "Clip p to [ε, 1−ε]"
              ],
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**Interview Point: why not MSE for classification?** Non-convex with sigmoid, weak gradients.",
                "**Which loss for outliers?** MAE or Huber."
              ],
              "style": "check"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
